WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
first let's take a look at the latest  scenes from kabul barbed wire has now  been put up outside the walls of kabul  airport but it hasn't stopped people  climbing the walls to try to get into  the airport and onto the runway earlier  people rushed onto the tarmac and tried  to climb onto moving military aircraft  and there are unconfirmed reports  that at least seven people died at the  airport  well people were also seen trying to  board commercial flights they have since  been suspended as well  [Music]  well us troops trying to secure the  airport fired shots into the air to try  to prevent hundreds of people running  onto the runways one official described  the crowd as out of control  meanwhile away from the airport on the  streets of kabul the taliban have set up  security checkpoints they're halting  traffic and checking inside cars  well let's go live to kabul and talk to  our chief correspondent stuart ramsey  there stuart first of all uh tell us  more about some those scenes we've been  witnessing today at the airport  yeah absolute chaos isn't it and it's  obviously resulted in the suspension of  this whole airlift and it's going to put  it back because commercial flights can't  come in either even if if they were able  to get some control we've heard uh  shooting uh pretty much throughout today  and helicopter gunships above us the  shooting we understand was actually uh  afghan forces trying to shoot over the  heads of people to get them to go away  from the gates of the airport we saw the  taliban there as well  trying to push people away shooting one  person we saw being shot at as after  they had got through uh the barricades  and of course those incredible pictures  are on the runway and of people uh  jumping onto planes and and and so  basically while they're still moving  incredible scenes and absolutely  incredible chaos now obviously something  had to be done so as you said they've  put over the wire and they're trying to  secure  or better secure the entire uh runway  but people of course are going it is a  huge complex and people will be  attempting to get over the wall  surrounded by a very high wall uh all  the time we suspect which is why there's  patrols going around you can probably  hear the shooting then that's that's the  same thing as they see people  approaching then they they fire uh in  the air so that suspension happened um  we're not certain when it's likely to  resume uh they'll have to presumably be  certain that they can control uh the  people this time because it has been  pretty awful um we've we've been hearing  from people saying who had tickets even  though those commercial flights didn't  happen but they had tickets they were  literally just pushed um out of the way  by people who had no paperwork uh of any  description i have heard reports that  one of the reasons for this at one of  another an airline i'm not sure which  one it was actually did just pretty much  let anyone on from the runway and that's  what's caused this because it's spread  like wildfire that if you can get  through the barricades then you can  get onto the  runway and then onto the onto the plane  of course the americans are now totally  in control of the whole airport or at  least nominally they are including the  air traffic control section but it was  this sort of a breakdown of law and  order outside and inside that's caused  such a delay now for the removal of  people and i think one thing that people  are beginning to come to to  understand and that includes the afghans  who have permission to travel to the  various countries they've been working  for is that if there's growing pressure  on this airlift then there'll be  priorities made and those priorities are  likely to take out the foreign nationals  and the afghans could easily be left  behind and for many of them they fear  that that is a death sentence and  while the country's  involved britain the united states but  others as well  are sorrowful about that  ultimately they will prioritize their  own nationals and  there could be some dreadful stories if  these people have to return to their  homes and the taliban start looking for  them which most people here are  convinced they're going to do and i  would just say on that point the we've  been uh interviewing uh the taliban  today number of fighters and they've  been saying to us that we're not going  to go after anyone that there is an  amnesty and that people can carry on uh  their lives as normal i think that's  what the everyone here is waiting to see  and the international community is  waiting to see is where the taliban  really have changed their ways i'll be  honest with you i'm somewhat skeptical  yeah because we're getting uh these  reports well we heard it from the un  secretary general of uh chilling human  rights abuses taking place in  afghanistan are you hearing anything  about what's going on on the streets  away from the airport  yeah well just quickly on that note uh  uh dermot over the last days that we've  been here i have seen a lot of videos  that we can't broadcast but it's dead  people basically who have been murdered  and laid out by the taliban and those  were either militia or people they  identified as having worked for either  foreign governments  or the afghan army and we've seen this  in towns and villages not so much in the  in the major cities now as it stands  today in kabul things are nowhere near  back to normal but as the day has  progressed more and more people have  come out onto the streets there's more  traffic uh you know bear in mind it was  absolutely deserted only a day or so ago  and um  shops have been opening but it is  absolutely awash with taliban fighters  they're everywhere um interestingly  excuse me interestingly they were  prepared to pose uh for our cameras and  give interviews now that i is incredible  i've never seen a change like that i  never thought i would see that uh where  they were quite happy to appear on  television you remember tv was banned uh  over 20 years ago and so um they they  are saying and bringing this this uh  position  that uh their leadership have tried to  sell to the international community that  it is a new look reformed taliban and  that while they will be strict they do  want it to be a more inclusive society  huge skepticism here by many people  certainly in the international community  i think one of the reasons they want to  do this obviously it is 20 years further  on and things have changed even for the  taliban but also they want to not just  have the support of say three countries  which i'm pretty certain it was when  they last had a government here but they  would actually like their neighbors for  example pakistan and russia example for  example to be supportive of them and on  board and to recognize them and for  other countries as well and you're only  going to do that if you do behave uh  responsibly and don't just start acting  like you're coming from the middle ages  for example and  that might be their plan the only thing  i would say to that is that might be  fine for the enlightened elite of the  taliban if you like the people who have  been involved in the negotiations  in qatar but for some of the  fighters um  they're very very conservative and they  have always ruled the areas uh  particularly in the provinces and the  regions which they've held for really  quite a long time never really lost it  to the coalition they've always had this  really hard core position  girls should stay at home they don't get  educated off the age of 11  and so on um whether they'll go down  that route we don't know will they  insist on burkas again most people seem  to think maybe not this time but we we  just don't know and so you have these  two things to where we have what is  going to happen in the future  the politics and the society all the  various issues but right now we've got  chaos at the airport this huge airlift  and it is a volatile situation it's  still very dangerous here  stuart ramsey live in kabul thank you

